WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.302
Every morning a kettle does something
clever, and you never notice.

00:00:04.002 --> 00:00:08.284
It heats water until the water boils, and
then, with nobody touching it, it stops.

00:00:09.165 --> 00:00:11.806
It has no clock to count
minutes and no screen to read,

00:00:12.266 --> 00:00:14.808
so how does a plain kettle
know the water is ready?

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:19.450
In many kettles, the answer begins with
something you can see rising from the spout.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.413
That cloud from the spout is
the clue the kettle uses too.

00:00:23.974 --> 00:00:26.096
Before the boil, only
a little steam forms,

00:00:26.337 --> 00:00:29.860
so a burst of it is a fast and
clear sign that the boil has begun.

00:00:30.401 --> 00:00:33.725
In many kettles, a small pipe
carries that steam to a sensor.

00:00:34.225 --> 00:00:38.149
The sensor sits in the steam and not in the
water, so it reacts to the boil itself.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:40.932
There is a bonus in that design.

00:00:41.232 --> 00:00:43.494
A little steam can't be
mistaken for a boil,

00:00:43.914 --> 00:00:46.937
so the sensor can be set to trip
well under one hundred degrees.

00:00:47.397 --> 00:00:50.100
That also helps on a mountain,
where water boils cooler.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:55.041
The sensor sitting in that steam is the
clever part, so what is it actually made of?

00:00:55.642 --> 00:00:58.203
Usually a small disc in the
shape of a shallow dish,

00:00:58.603 --> 00:01:01.964
made of two layers, each a
different metal, stuck together.

00:01:02.444 --> 00:01:04.085
Heat makes both layers expand,

00:01:04.385 --> 00:01:06.206
but one expands faster than the other,

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:08.907
and that mismatch makes the
whole disc bend as it warms.

00:01:09.647 --> 00:01:12.468
In other words, the kettle
turns heat into movement,

00:01:12.868 --> 00:01:14.809
but the bend by itself is gradual,

00:01:15.169 --> 00:01:18.070
and a gradual bend is not much
use for pulling a switch apart.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.852
So the kettle needs the bend
to turn into a sudden jerk.

00:01:22.273 --> 00:01:24.694
That is what the
shallow-dish shape is for:

00:01:25.135 --> 00:01:27.837
past its switching temperature
the disc does not keep bending,

00:01:28.137 --> 00:01:30.038
it flips all at once with a snap.

00:01:30.539 --> 00:01:32.920
In one common design,
the snap pushes a rod.

00:01:33.221 --> 00:01:35.783
The rod trips a lever
set up like a seesaw,

00:01:36.083 --> 00:01:38.184
which tips over once it
goes past the middle.

00:01:38.605 --> 00:01:41.407
Then the lever pulls the
electrical contacts apart,

00:01:41.707 --> 00:01:43.788
so they open sharply instead of slowly,

00:01:44.109 --> 00:01:45.630
and the power is cut cleanly.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:48.992
Everything so far assumes there is water,

00:01:49.392 --> 00:01:52.173
and that is a problem, because
with no water there is no boil,

00:01:52.494 --> 00:01:54.715
and with no boil there is
no steam to flip the disc.

00:01:55.335 --> 00:01:56.936
A separate part guards against that.

00:01:57.657 --> 00:02:00.739
In one design, it is a second
small disc that snaps,

00:02:01.319 --> 00:02:02.640
pressed against the heating element.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:04.721
When the element gets too hot,

00:02:05.041 --> 00:02:06.902
since no water is carrying the heat away,

00:02:07.463 --> 00:02:09.104
that disc trips and cuts the power.

00:02:10.104 --> 00:02:11.925
That design also adds a further backup,

00:02:12.606 --> 00:02:14.127
set to trip at a higher temperature,

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.528
which acts only if the guard itself fails,

00:02:17.089 --> 00:02:19.070
for example if its contacts weld shut.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:23.632
One last detail is that the kettle
has to be ready for the next morning.

00:02:24.333 --> 00:02:28.956
After a trip, the disc cools, and in one
design it springs back by itself once it

00:02:28.996 --> 00:02:32.118
has cooled about thirty degrees
below its switching temperature.

00:02:32.738 --> 00:02:34.840
In other designs, you press a reset knob.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.422
All in all, the story is simple.

00:02:38.063 --> 00:02:39.925
Steam tells the kettle the boil has come,

00:02:40.246 --> 00:02:42.488
and a bimetal disc turns
that heat into a bend.

00:02:43.049 --> 00:02:46.393
A snap makes the cut clean, and a
separate guard covers the empty kettle.

00:02:47.214 --> 00:02:51.460
Behind a whole morning ritual is two metals
growing at different speeds, and a snap.

00:02:52.701 --> 00:02:56.408
A disc that bends and snaps is a kind
of decision made by metal alone.

00:02:56.909 --> 00:02:59.113
There are more everyday
objects worth opening up,

00:02:59.373 --> 00:03:02.560
so the next part of the series looks
at another one. See you there.
